    feeds: ["anticipating failure modes", "deflating overconfidence", "surfacing dissent in groups", "strategic planning"],
    description: "Before committing to a plan, imagine it is 12 months later and the project has failed, then generate concrete reasons why. Mitchell, Russo & Pennington's controlled study found that adopting this 'prospective hindsight' frame increased the number of correctly identified reasons for a future outcome by about 30% versus ordinary forward-looking prediction. Klein popularized it as the team 'premortem' to surface risks that hierarchy and optimism suppress.",
