    description: "Randomized trials show colorectal screening prevents cancer and death, but the magnitude depends on the test. The UK Flexible Sigmoidoscopy trial (n=170,432) found a single sigmoidoscopy cut colorectal-cancer incidence 26% and mortality 30% over 17 years of follow-up. The NordICC colonoscopy RCT (n=84,585) found an 18% incidence reduction in intention-to-screen analysis, but its ~10-year colorectal-cancer mortality reduction was not statistically significant. Stool-based gFOBT/FIT programs reduce colorectal-cancer mortality roughly 15%.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice; discuss with a clinician. Colonoscopy's mortality benefit is less proven than its incidence benefit — NordICC's larger per-protocol effect (among those actually screened) is inflated by healthy-adherer bias, and colonoscopy carries small risks of perforation and bleeding.",
