    description: "The same option chosen or rejected depending on whether it is described as a gain or a loss, '90% survival' or '10% mortality.' Kühberger's (1998) meta-analysis of 136 papers and ~30,000 participants found a reliable but small-to-moderate framing effect (d ≈ 0.31); a later p-curve reappraisal corrected it upward to d ≈ 0.52. A person high in framing susceptibility has unstable preferences that a skilled communicator, marketer, or adversary can flip by re-wording.",
