    description: "Evaluating each decision in isolation instead of as part of the whole set, so locally reasonable choices sum to a globally bad or even dominated portfolio. Read, Loewenstein & Rabin (1999) formalized 'choice bracketing'; Rabin & Weizsäcker (2009) showed narrow bracketers make combinations of choices that are first-order stochastically dominated. Someone strong in this bias declines individually 'risky' positive-EV bets they should take as a set, and fails to pool risks — the mechanism behind myopic loss aversion.",
