    degrades: ["forecasting", "investment discipline", "trend judgment", "mean-reversion awareness"],
    description: "Over-weighting the most recent results and extrapolating them forward — buying whatever just went up. Frazzini & Lamont (2008) showed retail fund flows are 'dumb money': investors pour cash into funds holding recently hot stocks that then underperform, reducing their long-run wealth. Estimates put the cost of return-chasing at roughly 1.5-2% per year versus buy-and-hold. Someone strong in recency bias mistakes a hot streak for a durable trend and consistently buys high, sells low.",
