    description: "Gaslighting — systematically denying a partner's perceptions, memory, and feelings to make them doubt their own reality — is a tactic of coercive control, the most damaging subtype of intimate partner abuse. Lohmann et al.'s (2024) meta-analysis of 45 studies found coercive control moderately associated with PTSD (r = .32) and depression (r = .27); coercive control occurs in up to ~58% of IPV relationships and carries mental-health harm often exceeding non-embedded physical or situational abuse. Gaslighting specifically correlates with markedly lower self-esteem in targets.",
    callout: "'Gaslighting' as a construct is still loosely operationalized and popularly overextended; the coercive-control evidence rests largely on retrospective victim reports (mostly women), so shared-method and recall bias can inflate the associations — though the direction and clinical severity are not in doubt.",
