    description: "A persistent gap in sexual desire — one partner chronically wanting less, and withdrawing from initiation — is among the most commonly reported couple concerns and is negatively associated with both sexual and relationship satisfaction. Davies, Katz & Jackson (1999) found the link between desire discrepancy and *relationship* satisfaction was fully mediated by sexual satisfaction (i.e., discrepancy hurts mainly by lowering sexual satisfaction). Newer response-surface work (Kim et al., 2021) complicates the picture: higher absolute desire, more than a close match, predicts satisfaction.",
    callout: "Some desire discrepancy is near-universal and often benign; its effect on the relationship is indirect (through sexual satisfaction), and causality runs both ways — relationship dissatisfaction and stress suppress desire as much as low desire dents satisfaction.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Davies, S., Katz, J., & Jackson, J. L. (1999). Sexual desire discrepancies: Effects on sexual and relationship satisfaction in heterosexual dating couples. Archives of Sexual Behavior, 28(6), 553–567.", note: "Discrepancy's tie to relationship satisfaction fully mediated by sexual satisfaction. Limitation: dating (not long-term) couples, cross-sectional. [Moderate]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1018721417683", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Kim, J. J., Muise, A., Barranti, M., Mark, K. P., Rosen, N. O., Harasymchuk, C., & Impett, E. (2021). Are couples more satisfied when they match in sexual desire? New insights from response surface analyses. Social Psychological and Personality Science, 12(4), 487–496.", note: "Higher desire, not matching per se, predicted satisfaction — reframes 'discrepancy' as partly about overall desire level. Limitation: still largely cross-sectional dyadic data. [Moderate]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1177/1948550620926770", kind: "doi" },
    ],
