    description: "Investors pour money into funds and sectors AFTER they have risen and flee AFTER they fall, so their dollar-weighted returns trail the funds they own. Friesen & Sapp (2007) estimate poor market timing costs equity-fund investors ~1.56% per year (1991-2004), enough to offset the alpha of good funds. Frazzini & Lamont (2008) label these flows 'dumb money': stocks retail investors pile into subsequently underperform for about two years.",
