    degrades: ["Math and quantitative achievement", "Working memory available during problem-solving", "Willingness to pursue STEM paths"],
    description: "Apprehension about doing math depresses performance and drives avoidance of quantitative coursework and careers. Two large meta-analyses put the math-anxiety/achievement correlation at roughly r = -0.28 (Barroso et al., 223 studies) to r = -0.34 (Namkung et al., 131 studies). The mechanism Ashcraft identified is a transient 'affective drop': anxiety consumes working memory during timed, high-stakes math, so measured achievement underestimates true ability. Avoidance then reduces exposure and practice, compounding the gap over time.",
